  <span class="hero-ornament"></span>
  <h1 class="research-title">History Now</h1>
  <p class="ref-lede">What is being found and written about the past this week, the round anniversaries of the next thirty days, and what is new at the Institute.</p>
</header>

<section class="now-sec now-sec-news" id="news" aria-labelledby="now-news-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="now-news-t">In the news</h2>
  <p class="now-note">Headlines from other publications, newest first, each with the Institute&rsquo;s page on its subject. The headlines, the articles and the thumbnails their feeds offer belong to their publishers: the Institute has not written or checked them. A headline without a publisher&rsquo;s thumbnail shows the picture of the Institute&rsquo;s page instead. The list is refreshed twice a day.</p>
  <ol class="now-list" data-now="news" data-max="10"><li class="now-wait">The headlines need JavaScript.</li></ol>
  <p class="now-note now-src">From the news pages of <a href="https://archaeology.org/news/" rel="noopener">Archaeology Magazine</a>, <a href="https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/" rel="noopener">Smithsonian Magazine</a>, <a href="https://phys.org/science-news/archaeology/" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a>, <a href="https://archaeologymag.com/" rel="noopener">Archaeology News</a>, <a href="https://www.medievalists.net/" rel="noopener">Medievalists.net</a>, <a href="https://www.thehistoryblog.com/" rel="noopener">The History Blog</a>. A headline is listed only when it names a page of the Institute.</p>
</section>

<section class="now-sec" id="anniversaries" aria-labelledby="now-ann-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="now-ann-t">Coming anniversaries</h2>
  <p class="now-note">Round anniversaries in the next thirty days: centuries, half-centuries, quarter-centuries of the last three hundred years and the decades of the last hundred. Every day has its own page in <a href="/today-in-history/">Today in History</a>.</p>
  <ol class="now-list" data-now="ann" data-max="40"><li class="now-wait">The anniversaries need JavaScript.</li></ol>
</section>

<section class="now-sec" id="new" aria-labelledby="now-new-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="now-new-t">New at the Institute</h2>
  <ol class="wn-list">
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-16">16 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">The best-known entries, told in more detail</h3><p class="wn-s">Nearly 6,800 of the encyclopedia’s best-known entries (countries, cities, peoples and the people readers look for most) now go beyond their first paragraph: each has two or three further sections on its setting, its history and what remains, written from its source article and checked against it. This is the start of a longer effort to tell every entry in full.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/">The encyclopedia</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-16">16 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Past games: every earlier day of the daily games</h3><p class="wn-s">A new page lists every day of the eight daily games since each began, as a calendar. Choose a game and a day to play that day’s puzzle; the days already played show their result. The home page and the profile pages were also straightened: edges, cards and boxes of one row now line up.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/past-games">Past games</a> <a href="/games">History games</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-15">15 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Sources on nearly four thousand entries</h3><p class="wn-s">3,933 encyclopedia entries now end with a Sources section naming the scholarly works their Wikipedia article lists or cites, 9,518 works in all; a further 4,400 name the article they are written from. Only works about the subject itself, from scholarly publishers, are listed.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/method">How the Institute works</a> <a href="/corrections">Corrections</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-15">15 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Every great speech now has its words</h3><p class="wn-s">All 115 speeches now carry a text. Churchill's We Shall Fight on the Beaches, Mandela's statement from the dock, Jinnah's address to Pakistan's Constituent Assembly, Lloyd George at Limehouse, Stanton's Solitude of Self and Charles I on the scaffold are printed complete; the others have the passages that free sources give, each naming where its words come from.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/speeches/">Great speeches</a> <a href="/speeches/churchill-we-shall-fight">We Shall Fight on the Beaches</a> <a href="/speeches/mandela-statement-from-the-dock">Mandela's statement from the dock</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-14">14 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Wars and their battles, and the words of five founders</h3><p class="wn-s">A new page lists every war in the encyclopedia by region, oldest first, with its battles, sieges and treaties. The quotations page counts its speakers again and now holds sayings of Muhammad, verses of the Buddha, and passages of Laozi, Moses and Guru Nanak, each from a named edition. Entries, profiles and state pages ask whether the page was useful.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/wars">Wars and their battles</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-14">14 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">What is covered, and three corrections</h3><p class="wn-s">How the Institute works now shows what the encyclopedia covers by region and age, and where it is thin. The known disagreements between entries and their sources were checked against second sources: three entries corrected, the rest confirmed.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/method#coverage">How the Institute works</a> <a href="/corrections">Corrections</a></p></li>
  </ol>
  <p class="now-note"><a href="/new">Everything that is new, and every page added in the last thirty days &rarr;</a></p>
